---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Booking software for community pottery studios"
	description="Booking software for community and membership pottery studios: wheel time by membership tier, shared kiln firing queues, open-studio check-in and classes in one place."
>
	<section class="band-dark page-hero">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">For community studios</p>
				<h1>One studio, dozens of members, a few shared kilns.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					Membership studios run on goodwill and a whiteboard. Kilnbook keeps wheel time fair, the
					kiln queue moving and open-studio hours calm, so you can spend less time at the front desk.
				</p>
				<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
			</div>
			<img
				src="/images/studio-floor.svg"
				alt="A community studio floor with wheels, hand-building tables and a kiln room"
				width="560"
				height="360"
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Sound familiar?</p>
			<h2>What gets hard as a membership studio grows</h2>
			<div class="cards">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>The same few members get the wheels</h3>
					<p>
						Whoever arrives first on a Tuesday evening gets a wheel. Members who work late or have
						kids miss out, and start asking why they pay the same.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Nobody knows what is in the kiln</h3>
					<p>
						Work piles up on the bisque shelf, firing fees are counted by hand, and half the
						questions at the desk are "is my bowl out yet?"
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Open studio is a guessing game</h3>
					<p>
						You cannot tell who is in the building, who has used up their hours, or whether tonight
						is about to be packed.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Fair wheel time for every membership tier</h2>
		<p>
			Members reserve wheels, hand-building tables and shared equipment like the slab roller in
			one-hour blocks. You decide how many hours each tier gets, how far ahead they can book, and
			whether evenings and weekends count double. Cancellation windows and no-show rules free up
			slots for someone else. <a href="/features/wheel-time">How wheel time works</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>A firing queue for your shared kilns</h2>
		<p>
			Members log a piece from their phone when they set it on the shelf and pay the firing fee by
			cubic inch, per piece or both. Each kiln has its own bisque and glaze queue, so you can see
			how full every load is. When a load comes out, everyone with work in it gets a pickup text.
			<a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Open-studio hours without the clipboard</h2>
		<p>
			Put a tablet by the door. Members tap their name when they arrive, so you always know who is
			in the building, which matters when the kilns are running.
		</p>

		<h2>Classes that bring in new members</h2>
		<p>
			Most community studios grow through classes. Sell six-week wheel courses, drop-in sessions
			and class packs, with waitlists that fill cancelled seats on their own. Students who finish a
			course are already in Kilnbook when they are ready to become members.
			<a href="/features/classes">Classes and packs</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Which plan fits a community studio?</h2>
		<p>
			Most community studios start on the Studio plan at $79 a month. It covers up to 3 kilns with
			unlimited members, wheel and table reservations, class packs, waitlists and memberships, and
			pickup texts. Running more kilns or a second location? Collective has no kiln limit.
			<a href="/pricing">Compare plans</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Give your members a studio that runs itself.</h2>
			<p>30 days free on the Studio plan. No card to start, and nothing is deleted if you walk away.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
